    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks!</p>\n<p>What exactly does it mean to declare an instance? I notice that, if I remove the <code>(H : Subgroup G)</code> binder from the instance declaration, and rename every reference to <code>H</code> in the definition of <code>quotient_map</code> as <code>W</code>... there's no problem.</p>\n<p>My mental model of an \"instance\" is that it's a tangible (in some sense) artifact that exists taking up space somewhere, but this declaration appears to be more in the vein of declaring that such an artifact <em>could</em> exist, if you wanted it. Is that right?</p>",
        "id": 603878222,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1781634586
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Whenever you use a declaration that has a parameter with binders <code>[...]</code>, Lean will try to automatically fill this parameter through an algorithm called instance synthesis. To do so it looks at all the <code>instance</code> declared that could work until it finds one that does. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Hom/Defs.html#MonoidHom\">MonoidHom</a> has a <code>[MulOne _]</code> argument, which is filled in by instance synthesis. See <a href=\"https://leanprover.github.io/theorem_proving_in_lean4/Type-Classes/#type-classes\">this chapter</a> of <a href=\"https://leanprover.github.io/theorem_proving_in_lean4/\">#tpil</a>.</p>",
        "id": 603882104,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1781635472
    }
]